---
title: Japanese Chicken Rice in Rice Cooker
servings: 2
course: dinner
cuisine: Japanese
tags: quick, one-pan, make-ahead, chicken, rice
image: https://www.marmiton.org/assets/images/default-recipe-picture_80x80-6PRRwF3.jpg
source: https://www.marmiton.org/recettes/recette_riz-poulet-japonais-au-rice-cooker_224166.aspx
prep time: 10m
cook time: 45m
---

-- a serving here is a whole meal for someone who does not eat breakfast: about 1290 kcal.
-- Two of these a day is the 2500 kcal target.

-- the source is 4 rice-cooker cups of rice to 200 g of chicken, which is a plate of rice with a
-- garnish — 73% of its calories were carbohydrate. This turns that round: less rice, three times
-- the chicken, and peas, which is mame gohan territory and the only fibre in the bowl.

-- everything goes in cold and the machine does the rest. The only work is the chopping.

Wash the @sushi rice{350%g} until the water runs clear. Soaking it for ~{15%minutes} gives a better grain, but skip it if you want to eat sooner — chop while it sits.

Cut the @chicken thigh{700%g} into bite-sized pieces, the @carrot{1} into matchsticks and the @mushroom{150%g} into slices.

-- boneless thigh fillet is $24.99/kg at Royal Oak and bone-in thighs are $7.99. Bone-in laid on
-- top whole is far cheaper, needs no cutting at all, and the meat pulls off with two forks once
-- it is cooked. Allow half again the weight for the bones.

Put the @&sushi rice{}, @soy sauce{1.5%tbsp}, @mirin{4%tbsp}, @sugar{1.5%tbsp}, @water{300%ml}, @&mushroom{} and @&carrot{} into the #rice cooker{}. Stir once so the sugar dissolves and the rice sits flat.

-- the source uses 50 ml of soy. Soy sauce is 5.8 g of sodium per 100 g, so at this portion size
-- that alone was over 3 g of salt a meal; 1.5 tbsp keeps it near 2.5 and the mirin carries the rest.
-- Add more at the table if you want it, where you can taste what you are adding.

Scatter @frozen pea{200%g} over the rice — straight from the bag, no defrosting — and lay the @&chicken thigh{} on top without stirring it in. The chicken steams above the rice and its juices run down through it.

Start the #rice cooker{} on the normal setting and leave it, about ~{45%minutes}. Do not open the lid.

Fluff it together so the chicken, peas and vegetables run through the rice, and scatter over @?spring onion{2}(sliced) and @?sesame seed{1%tsp}.

-- what you do not eat tonight gets eaten over the next day or two. Spread it out to cool rather
-- than leaving the pot warm, fridge it within the hour, and reheat covered in the #microwave{}
-- with a splash of water. Rice left sitting warm is the one leftover worth being careful with.
